p('Useful spectral replacements and incidence stopping bounds for QC-GHP codes','Title2')
p('24 October 2026 (Pacific) | Expanded stronger-bound follow-up to L-F16-stopping-bound','Small2')
p('<b>Abstract.</b> Useful spectral replacements exist on explicit sparse QC-GHP families. Combining the classical fourth-moment cycle identity with the column-overlap bound yields stopping distance at least γ+1 whenever the degree-corrected fourth singular-value moment has zero excess. Explicit joint Sidon circulants satisfy the exact spectral equality, binary CSS commutation, fixed degrees and nontrivial logical content. The criterion needs neither the original second-eigenvalue gap nor a separate small-size exclusion. These ingredients are already available in classical literature and the motivating paper; we supply checked corollaries, explicit parameters and source-to-matrix bridges. Separately, a two-block incidence criterion gives 2(γ+σ)≤(σ+τ)|S|, an explicit weight-four/eight family has exact stopping and logical distance six, and Gram-equal CSS twins prove an unbounded obstruction to uniformly informative Gram-only bounds. All new proofs are checked in Lean.')
table([['Question / source','Answer and scope'],['Abiad-Castriota,15 July 2026, v1,Prop. 5.1,Remark 5.2,§6: useful parameters for the stated bound','Answered negatively by the previous universal theorem; unchanged here.'],['Same source: tighter spectral stopping bounds, including multi-eigenvalue refinements','YES for useful-replacement existence: a fourth-moment criterion proves γ+1 on explicit sparse joint Sidon families. A separate Gram obstruction limits uniform improvement on other families.'],['This requested follow-up: incidence-conditioned replacement exceeding two on a sparse family beyond B1','YES. A reusable bound, explicit infinite families, nontrivial logical content, and a separate exact-six family.']])
p('The historical source question and this project-derived target are kept distinct. This paper adds no completed canonical author-question to the inventory. The proofs are published at an immutable source revision; all checked Lean files, including audit programs and dependencies, have full current and pinned URLs below.','Small2')
page();h('1. A useful fourth-moment spectral replacement')
p('Write η₁,…,ηₘ for all ordered eigenvalues of the actual unnormalised real Gram matrix HHᵀ, counting multiplicity. If a binary incidence matrix has column degrees dᵥ and row degrees r_c, its fourth singular-value moment satisfies the zero-excess condition')
eq('Σᵢηᵢ² + Σᵥdᵥ = Σᵥdᵥ² + Σ꜀r_c².')
p('<b>Theorem 1.</b> Under this equality, if every column degree is at least γ, every nonempty stopping support has size at least γ+1. For a (γ,k)-regular matrix with N columns and m rows, the equality is Σᵢηᵢ²+Nγ=Nγ²+mk², equivalently Σᵢηᵢ²=Nγ(γ+k−1), using mk=Nγ. No condition on η₂ and no presumed exclusion of small supports occurs.')
p('<b>Proof.</b> The checked spectral theorem identifies Σηᵢ² with tr((HHᵀ)²), then with the sum of squared entries of HᵀH. Let qᵤᵥ be the natural number of common checks of columns u,v. The diagonal is dᵥ, and Σᵤᵥqᵤᵥ=Σ꜀r_c². The displayed condition is exactly Σᵤ≠ᵥ(qᵤᵥ²−qᵤᵥ)=0. Each summand is nonnegative because qᵤᵥ is a natural number. Thus every distinct-column intersection is at most one. Covering the γ neighbors of a selected column by its intersections with the other selected columns gives γ≤|S|−1. SpectralMoment.lean and MomentBound.lean check the ordered-eigenvalue identity, binary/cardinality bridges, finite sums, zero-excess implication and final stopping theorem.')
p('This is a corollary of known ingredients: the motivating source [1], Lemma4.8, gives the cycle/moment identity, and Kashyap-Vardy [2], Theorem1, gives the overlap bound; Xia-Fu [8], Theorem3, also supplies the minimum-column-weight formulation. We do not claim a new classical counting principle. The precise existence interpretation of the §6 stronger-replacement direction is therefore positive; optimal universal refinements are a different target.')
if any(e['local_path']=='MomentReplacement.lean'for e in d['paper_lean_files']):
 p('<b>A globally valid replacement.</b> For positive column weight γ, define L from (m,N,γ,k,Ση²) to be γ+1 when the regular moment equality holds and2 otherwise. MomentReplacement.lean proves L≥2 and L≤|S| for every nonempty stopping support of every positive-column-weight regular binary matrix. The fallback is proved from positive columns, rather than assuming away small supports. GlobalMomentFamily.lean proves L=γ+1, hence L&gt;2 for γ≥2, on every constructed family member. Thus this is an actual bound function valid outside its informative regime too.')
if any(e['local_path']=='MomentExcess.lean'for e in d['paper_lean_files']):
 p('<b>Quantitative excess extension.</b> Let Δ=Ση²+Σdᵥ−Σdᵥ²−Σr_c². For any natural μ with Δ&lt;2μ(μ+1), every stopping support satisfies γ+μ≤μ|S| when the minimum column degree is γ. MomentExcess.lean proves this without regularity or an assumed intersection bound: an ordered distinct-column pair with overlap q contributes2q(q−1) to the nonnegative total excess, forcing q≤μ. The uniform covering theorem then applies. This supplies a checked criterion for small nonzero excess; it does not assert optimality or construct a new infinite family with prescribed nonzero excess.')
h('1.1 Explicit QC-GHP parameters satisfying the replacement')
p('Fix an even γ≥2 and any odd n≥3<super>2γ</super>. Over Z/n take S={3ⁱ:0≤i&lt;γ} and T={3<super>γ+i</super>:0≤i&lt;γ}. Let A and B be their binary circulants. Use the source scalar-factor convention')
eq('H<sub>X</sub>=[A|Bᵀ], &nbsp; H<sub>Z</sub>=[B|Aᵀ].')
p('The combined support S∪T is Sidon, including repeated summands, with no reduction of any relevant pair sum modulo n. The two supports are disjoint. Thus distinct columns meet in at most one check. Circulants commute, giving H<sub>X</sub>H<sub>Z</sub>ᵀ=ABᵀ+BᵀA=0 over F₂. There are n checks and 2n variables; each column has weight γ and each row weight2γ. The actual spectral equality is')
eq('Σᵢηᵢ² = 2nγ(3γ−1), &nbsp; hence s(H<sub>X</sub>)≥γ+1.')
p('The useful bound is proved again through Theorem1, with the constructed spectral identity as input. JointSidon.lean, JointMatrices.lean, JointPowers.lean and JointMomentFamily.lean check all hypotheses and the matrix/source bridge. Both binary kernel sectors also have nonzero weight at least γ+1 (JointKernel.lean). The all-ones-left word has zero X-syndrome and odd left parity, while every Z-stabilizer combination has even left parity; the code therefore has a nontrivial logical class. For fixed even γ, n=3<super>2γ</super>(2t+1) gives an infinite sparse family with fixed degrees. In particular γ=2 gives column/row weights2/4 and stopping guarantee3, beyond the earlier B1 family. No encoded-dimension formula or positive-rate claim is made.')
h('1.2 Short support parameters and a54-variable certificate')
p('<b>Fixed degree4/8 family.</b> Take S={0,1,3,11} and T={0,5,12,18} modulo any odd n≥37, and use H<sub>X</sub>=[A|Bᵀ],H<sub>Z</sub>=[B|Aᵀ]. Then N=2n, the column/row weights are4/8, Ση²=88n, and the globally valid spectral replacement equals5. Every nonempty X-stopping support and every nonzero kernel word in either CSS sector has weight at least5. The all-ones-left class is nontrivial. Thus this is a fixed-degree infinite sparse family starting at74 variables, with no exponential sufficient-length restriction. Exact stopping distance and an encoded-dimension formula are not claimed.')
p('<b>Proof and classical construction comparison.</b> Each support separately is Sidon, and mixed sums s+t are unique with their ordered support roles. SeparateSidon.lean proves these weaker hypotheses suffice for column intersections≤1; disjoint supports and a Sidon union are unnecessary. ShortFamilySupports.lean kernel-checks the finite natural support identities and proves the no-wrap bridge for all n≥37, including cardinalities. Every relevant pair sum is at most36. ShortFamily.lean proves the actual binary CSS, exact moment, useful bound function, both-sector kernel bounds, nontrivial logical class and unbounded lengths. This is an explicit realization of the motivating source [1],Corollary4.11, which characterizes girth≥6 by combined nonzero difference multiplicities≤1. The source criterion and its classical design terminology receive credit.')
p('<b>Smaller finite instance.</b> The same displayed supports also work modulo27, although the no-wrap sufficient condition does not apply. Practical27Core.lean checks every distinct-column overlap of the actual54-variable matrix in Lean’s kernel. Practical27.lean gives Ση²=2376, stopping bound5 and a nontrivial logical class; Practical27Design.lean verifies the separate/mixed conditions and both-sector kernel bounds. This is an informative stopping-distance guarantee, not merely a spectral inequality. It is a lower bound, not an exact-five theorem. The finite search that suggested the supports is not a premise.')
h('2. The two-block stopping bound')
p('Let E(v,c) be a finite binary incidence relation. A stopping support S is nonempty and no check has exactly one selected neighbor. It need not be connected, elementary, or a stabilizer support. Partition the columns by a Boolean color. Suppose every selected column has at least γ neighbors, distinct selected columns of the same color have at most σ common neighbors, and columns of different colors have at most τ common neighbors, with σ≤τ. These hypotheses may be imposed globally to obtain a stopping-distance guarantee.')
p('<b>Theorem 2.</b> Under these assumptions,')
eq('2(γ+σ) ≤ (σ+τ)|S|.')
p('If σ+τ&gt;0 this gives the integer bound ceiling(2(γ+σ)/(σ+τ)). The division-free statement also handles zero intersection limits. Both parts are allowed to be empty individually; nonemptiness is required only for S.')
p('<b>Proof.</b> Fix a selected column v. Every check incident with v must meet another selected column. Covering its neighbors by the intersections with the other selected columns gives γ≤Σ<sub>w∈S,w≠v</sub>|N(v)∩N(w)|. Let p and q count selected columns in the two parts. A selected left column therefore gives γ+σ≤σp+τq; a selected right column gives γ+σ≤τp+σq. If both parts occur, add these inequalities. If only one occurs, the corresponding inequality gives γ+σ≤σ|S|, which is at least as strong because τ≥σ. This proves the claim. All covering, finite-sum, partition and one-part cases are checked in PartitionBound.lean.')
p('<b>Classical comparison.</b> Kashyap-Vardy [2], Theorem 1, assumes uniform column size w and maximum distinct-column intersection μ; its bound is 1+w/μ. The same count appears in the checked division-free theorem γ+μ≤μ|S|. Retaining the two parts refines this classical argument. At γ=4,σ=1,τ=2, the uniform criterion gives three and Theorem 2 gives four. The exact-six result below uses additional finite incidence information.')
h('3. Sidon supports give verified incidence hypotheses')
p('For a finite abelian group G, call a support T Sidon when equality a+b=c+d among its elements implies equality of the unordered pairs, including repeated elements. Let A be the binary circulant with A<sub>c,j</sub>=1 exactly when c−j∈T, and set')
eq('H<sub>X</sub>=H<sub>Z</sub>=[A | Aᵀ].')
p('These are actual binary matrices with |G| rows and 2|G| columns. Their two column neighborhoods are j+T and j−T. A Sidon identity shows that distinct columns within one part share at most one check; across parts they share at most two. For the latter statement, after one common check c₀ is fixed, the only possible common checks are c₀ and v+w−c₀. The formal proof includes cardinality and empty-intersection cases. Every column has |T| neighbors and every row has 2|T| neighbors.')
p('Circulants commute with their transposes. Hence H<sub>X</sub>H<sub>Z</sub>ᵀ=AAᵀ+AᵀA=0 over F₂. This is precisely the scalar QC-GHP/generalized-bicycle form of [1,4], with r₁=n₁=1 and b=a. Applying Theorem 2 gives 3|S|≥2(|T|+1), without any spectral or girth assumption.')
h('4. An explicit sparse family for every fixed guarantee')
p('<b>Theorem 3.</b> Fix an even integer γ≥2. For any odd n≥3<super>γ</super>, use G=Z/n and')
eq('T={1,3,3²,…,3<super>γ−1</super>}, &nbsp; a(x)=Σ<sub>i=0</sub><super>γ−1</super>x<super>3ⁱ</super>,<br/>H<sub>X</sub>=H<sub>Z</sub>=[A | Aᵀ].')
p('Then the matrices are binary CSS checks of column weight γ and row weight 2γ. Every nonempty stopping support has size at least ceiling(2(γ+1)/3). The quantum code has a nontrivial logical class. For fixed γ, n=3<super>γ</super>(2t+1),t≥0, gives arbitrarily long matrices with the same degrees.')
p('<b>Proof of the support condition.</b> Sort the exponents in 3<super>a</super>+3<super>b</super>=3<super>c</super>+3<super>d</super>, with a≤b and c≤d. If b&lt;d, the left side is at most2·3<super>b</super>, whereas the right side is at least3<super>d</super>≥3·3<super>b</super>, a contradiction. The opposite inequality is symmetric; cancel the equal largest terms to identify the smaller exponents. Every relevant pair sum is below n, since2·3<super>γ−1</super>&lt;3<super>γ</super>≤n. Thus reduction modulo n preserves this Sidon property and all γ marks remain distinct. PowersThree.lean checks the inequalities, no-wrap cast bridge and cardinality; the support is not assumed Sidon as an unproved input.')
p('<b>Nontrivial quantum content.</b> Take the binary word equal to one on every left variable and zero on the right. Its syndrome is zero because every row meets the left block in the even number γ. The sum of its left entries is one because n is odd. Every companion row has left sum zero, and so does every linear combination of companion rows. The word is therefore outside the stabilizer span. SidonMatrices.lean checks the finite-sum, binary-field and span-separation argument.')
table([['Fixed column / row weights','New guarantee / classical uniform guarantee'],['4 / 8','4 / 3'],['6 / 12','5 / 4'],['8 / 16','6 / 5'],['General even γ / 2γ','ceiling(2(γ+1)/3) / ceiling(γ/2+1)']])
p('Choosing γ first gives any desired fixed guarantee, and then length may increase with degrees fixed. This is not a claim of growing stopping distance at fixed degree, positive asymptotic rate, or practical parameter efficiency. The displayed sufficient length is exponential in γ. The known companion-row stopping mechanism gives a size2γ upper witness; CSSStopping.lean checks that mechanism on the same binary matrices.')
p('A second parameter choice makes the guarantee grow: γ=2d and n=3<super>2d</super> give stopping and nonzero-kernel weight at least d+1. GrowingFamily.lean proves (2d)3<super>d</super>≤n, so the incidence density is at most3<super>−d</super>. KernelFamily.lean supplies the binary/kernel bridge. This varies the degree with length; it does not assert growing stopping distance at fixed degree.')
h('5. A separate family with exact stopping and logical distance six')
p('<b>Theorem 4.</b> For every positive integer t, let n=39t and use a(x)=1+x+x⁴+x⁶ with H<sub>X</sub>=H<sub>Z</sub>=[A|Aᵀ]. There are 78t variables and 39t checks, column weight 4 and row weight 8. The stopping distance and the quantum logical distance are both exactly six.')
p('The reusable intersection criterion proves four for this entire family. The stronger exact result is certified independently: simultaneous coordinate translation reduces the base n=39 search to the two side representatives. A sound degree-one extension procedure rejects both roots with four further additions, excluding all stopping supports of size at most five. Its exact Boolean tests are reduced by Lean’s kernel. No external search result is a premise.')

p('Reduction modulo39 maps lifted column lists onto their base lists, lifts every incident base check, and is injective on each check’s neighbor set. The checked local-cover theorem therefore projects a stopping support to a base stopping support of no greater size. This transfers the lower bound six to every positive39-multiple lift; it is not extrapolation from sampled lengths.')
p('A six-variable upper witness is')
eq('left(0), left(−5), left(−4),<br/>right(1), right(5), right(6).')
p('The six variables are distinct at every stated lift. Every incident check has a selected partner. A separate exact parity calculation shows that every selected check degree is even; the binary word is in the kernel. It has left weight three, whereas each companion row has left weight four. The same parity functional excludes every stabilizer combination. Every nonzero binary kernel word has stopping support, so the lower stopping bound gives logical weight at least six; the nontrivial six-word gives equality in both CSS sectors, which coincide here. Unlike the completed B1 family, this upper witness is a logical word rather than a companion row. It is also not assumed elementary: the independent base reconstruction has selected degrees zero, two and four.')
p('BicycleMatrices.lean proves the actual circulant/transpose binary matrix entries equivalent to the incidence relation and checks CSS commutation. BicycleDegrees.lean proves dimensions and degrees. The translation, kernel certificate, cover, cardinality, syndrome and stabilizer-span bridges are all included in the proof chain. The statement does not assert a formula for the logical dimension. Independent binary elimination gives k=6 for the first three sampled lifts; that is a computation recorded separately, not a new general rank theorem.')
h('6. What the full real Gram matrix cannot certify')
p('<b>Theorem 5.</b> For the same constituent circulant A, define')
eq('Good: &nbsp; H<sub>X</sub>=H<sub>Z</sub>=[A|Aᵀ];<br/>Bad: &nbsp; H′<sub>X</sub>=[A|A], &nbsp; H′<sub>Z</sub>=[Aᵀ|Aᵀ].')
p('Both pairs are binary CSS checks whose X-matrices have the same numbers of rows and columns and the same row/column degrees. No equality of encoded quantum dimension is assumed or claimed. Over the reals,')
eq('H<sub>X</sub>H<sub>X</sub>ᵀ=AAᵀ+AᵀA=2AAᵀ=H′<sub>X</sub>H′<sub>X</sub>ᵀ.')
p('The bad X-matrix has two identical positive columns at each coordinate, yielding a stopping pair. Positive columns exclude singletons, so its stopping distance is exactly two. The good family of Theorem 4 has distance six. More generally the same identity holds for every support in Theorem 3, together with a bad stopping pair.')
p('Let L be any proposed universally valid real-valued lower-bound function whose inputs consist only of this real Gram matrix and the shared check-matrix dimensions/degrees. Validity on the bad counterpart forces L≤2; identical input data force the same output on the good code. GramObstruction.lean, BadStopping.lean, SidonBad.lean and RealGramBound.lean prove this statement against the actual matrices and stopping supports. Any function of the complete Gram spectrum, or of eigenvectors determined solely by that Gram matrix, is included. An additional incidence hypothesis may distinguish the codes and is precisely what the replacement uses.')
p('<b>Unbounded factor loss.</b> Fix M≥1 and use the paired powers family with γ=4M and n=3<super>4M</super>(2t+1). Every good stopping support has size at least2M+1; the bad twin has stopping distance2. Therefore any real-valued Gram-only lower bound valid on the bad member satisfies M·L&lt;|S| for every good stopping support. GramGap.lean checks this statement with the exact matrices and all quantifiers. The bad weight-two word is outside the companion stabilizer span; BadLogical.lean checks both sector lower bounds and the nontrivial logical witness. These are not zero-information quantum examples. Binary rank and encoded dimension remain additional data outside the obstruction.')
if any(e['local_path']=='BothGram.lean'for e in d['paper_lean_files']):
 p('BothGram.lean further proves equality of the two separate real check Gram matrices and the corresponding bound obstruction. This does not include cross-sector real incidence data, the variable-side eigenvectors of the original matrices, or binary ranks.')
h('7. Exact comparison with spectral and known stopping bounds')
p('The motivating source [1], Proposition 5.1, requires σ₂(H<sub>X</sub>)²&lt;γ/2 and separately excludes the small root branch before concluding a stopping-distance lower bound. The preceding universal parameter theorem answers that proposition’s useful-parameter question negatively in its stated scalar-factor model. That answer is preserved. Theorem 2 replaces its spectral step with incidence information; it does not claim parameters satisfying the source inequality.')
p('Zhu-Chugg [3], Corollary 6, equations 41-42, give the two regular Tanner-graph expressions below, with N variables, column degree γ, row degree k and second Gram eigenvalue η:')
eq('N(2γ−η)/(γk−η),<br/>2N(2γ+k−2−η)/(k(γk−η)).')
p('Their regular connected setting and strict gap η&lt;γk must be respected. On every39-cover of the exact-six family, an actual two-dimensional rational Rayleigh certificate proves η≥290/13. Its base vectors have disjoint supports, squared norms13, energies332 and cross energy42. The minimum plane quotient is (332−42)/13. SpectralPlane.lean proves the ordered-second-eigenvalue conclusion; FiberSums.lean and BicycleCoverEnergy.lean prove exact norm/energy scaling through the cyclic cover; BicycleSpectralFamily.lean proves the family result. Consequently both Zhu-Chugg expressions are strictly negative whenever η&lt;32. If the gap fails, those formulas are outside their stipulated regime. No floating-point estimate is a proof premise.')
p('On the new joint-support instance γ=2,n=81, the fourth-moment certificate is1620 and Theorem1 gives3. JointSpectralBase.lean checks another rational plane certificate: squared norms40, energies280 and cross energy38 give η≥121/20&gt;6. Under η&lt;8, both Zhu-Chugg expressions are strictly negative. Thus the displayed spectral replacement is informative on a concrete instance where those two formulas are not. Other applicability hypotheses of the cited theorem are not discarded by this algebraic comparison.')
p('The54-variable instance has another actual ordered-second-eigenvalue certificate: u=δ₀+δ₈−δ₄−δ₁₂ and v=δ₂₀+δ₁−δ₂₄−δ₅ have disjoint supports, squared norms4, energies34 and cross energy0, proving η≥17/2. Under η&lt;32, the first Zhu-Chugg expression is negative, and the second is at most297/94&lt;4, whereas the moment replacement gives5. Practical27Spectral.lean checks all integer evaluations, real casts, the eigenvalue conclusion and both formula comparisons. This preserves the cited theorem’s other applicability hypotheses.')
p('For illustration only, independent reconstruction gives η≈27.52509838 for n=39, and η≈7.53208889 for the joint n=81 code. Exact integer checks cover eight39-lifts, twelve joint-family parameter choices and all certificate numerators. Sampled ranks and floating-point eigenvalues remain diagnostics, separate from every theorem.')
p('Kashyap-Vardy [2] supplies the classical uniform-overlap criterion already sufficient for a positive answer exceeding two in the weight-four case. Theorem 2 improves its value by retaining the block partition. Schwartz-Vardy [5] emphasizes that stopping distance belongs to a specified parity-check matrix and that codeword supports are stopping sets. Their stopping redundancy instead optimizes over added checks; that problem is different from our fixed-H<sub>X</sub> guarantee. The generic binary kernel/stopping bridge is checked here.')
p('Wang-Pryadko [4], Eq.8, uses H<sub>X</sub>=[A|B],H<sub>Z</sub>ᵀ=[B;−A]; our paired construction takes B=Aᵀ. Their distance bounds and gcd dimension formula concern logical code parameters. A logical-distance lower bound alone does not exclude non-codeword stopping supports. Kuo-Ouyang [6] studies decoders exploiting degeneracy; success of such a decoder does not establish a lower bound for ordinary peeling stopping sets. The known stabilizer upper mechanism and the new nontrivial logical upper witness are distinguished above.')
p('Dehghan-Banihashemi [9] gives earlier cospectral bipartite examples with matched degree sequences and different cycle counts. Their inspected cycle statements explain why spectral information can omit incidence data; they are not the actual full-Gram-equal binary CSS family and stopping/logical certificates proved here. The existing F38 full-text screen and its two recorded directions are reused, without inventing a new inventory cohort.')
p('Xia-Fu [8], Theorem3, assumes minimum column weight γ, maximum distinct-column intersection λ and integral γ/λ. It gives the pseudo-weight bound γ/λ+1 and an equality characterization. At γ=4,λ=2 it gives3, whereas the two-block refinement gives4. Its girth≥6 Theorem1 is inapplicable to the paired family’s cross-block4-cycles. These hypotheses, pseudo-weight versus stopping distance, and the two matrix families are kept distinct.')
p('Public GitHub comparisons include the pinned bicycle-race README, which describes logical-distance records, and the Kuo decoder repository metadata. A final CLI support-fingerprint query and a known-positive mathlib control both returned no items; those negative code-search outputs are discarded. Authenticated Edge access later succeeded: the arXiv-ID query visibly returned the source announcement and an earlier project lift-label post. The source post detail displayed10:23PM,15July2026; the prior UTC16July date is distinguished in the chronology. A focused QC-GHP stopping query displayed no results in that bounded view. None is a proof of global absence or priority. The earlier Mac-locked failure is superseded by this fresh read-only check. No post was sent.')

p('The post-lemma audit also inspected Donovan-Rao-Üsküplü-Yazıcı [10], whose Lemma9 gives stopping distance≥8 when3 does not divide a for their specified DM/DCA parity-check matrices, with four disjoint row groups and the source construction hypotheses. Those classical matrices and their stopping patterns are compared as stated; no QC-GHP companion/CSS bridge is supplied by that result for our displayed matrices. Chee-Ling-Yin [11] distinguishes cyclic difference packing from the additional partition requirement of PCDP; our overlapping pair of supports is not a claimed PCDP. Gholami-Samadieh [12] studies classical lifted finite set systems and inevitable walks. Amirzade-Sadeghi [13] gives classical single/multiple-edge difference criteria and lifting-degree bounds; it supplies no stated QC-GHP stopping or CSS-companion theorem for these matrices. These are concrete construction comparisons, not evidence that classical stopping bounds above two are new.')
